# Microphone forwarding

Status: implementation and all four 1.0.156 candidate builds pass, including
signed/notarized macOS packages. End-to-end hardware qualification remains.
Not merged, released or production-installed.
Branch: microphone-forwarding, based on raptorq-upgrade root aee9431 and
Client 5132482f. This retains all security/lifecycle fixes, RaptorQ 2.0.1,
datagram-pacer removal and macOS profile-label cleanup. Do not merge to main
or replace the matching 1.0.154 rollback candidates during development.

## Agreed product behavior

- Linux and macOS Clients send the OS-selected microphone to a macOS Host.
  Linux Host microphone injection is not part of this phase.
- Audio Settings has two independent Automatic/Manual choices, both default
  Automatic: Microphone activation; Select PLANK Microphone as Host sound input.
- Automatic activation starts only with an authenticated, microphone-capable
  session and OS consent. Manual starts off until the toolbar button is used.
- One compact toolbar microphone button displays active/off/unavailable state,
  provides enable/mute and a visible state/hover hint. Settings expose accessible
  labels; the existing native painted toolbar is not a screen-reader control tree.
  Never imply recording
  succeeded when permission, device access or Host delivery failed.
- Off means no Client microphone capture. Disconnect ends capture. Temporary
  reconnects and login/desktop transitions preserve the user's mute choice.
- Automatic input selection saves the previous Host input device UID, selects
  PLANK Microphone while the session owns it, and restores on final teardown
  only if PLANK Microphone is still selected. Do not override later user changes.
  Mute supplies silence; it must never switch to the physical Host microphone.
- Manual selection publishes the virtual device without changing the default.
  An application's explicit input selection remains under that application's
  control. Device-unplug/crash/worker-replacement cleanup must be bounded.
- Existing Host-to-Client audio is independent and must continue unchanged.

## Architecture and constraints

1. Client captures through SDL3 (PipeWire on Linux, Core Audio on macOS), using
   the OS-selected recording source. Permission requests remain normal OS UI;
   never grant or modify TCC databases. Capture begins only after authorization.
2. Mono float PCM at 48 kHz is encoded as bounded short Opus packets. Carry
   sequence/timestamps and explicit activation generations; late pre-mute audio
   cannot play after unmute, reconnect, takeover or user switch.
3. Negotiate a reverse KyProto audio endpoint on the existing authenticated
   QUIC connection. No new port, tunnel, legacy transport or reliable media
   backlog. Keep control messages bounded and capability-gate the feature.
4. Host decodes outside Core Audio's real-time callback. Bound packet sizes,
   queue duration, jitter and clock drift. Silence on starvation, stale producer,
   teardown or invalid state; no delayed playback of old captured speech.
5. Publish a minimal AudioServerPlugIn input-only device named PLANK Microphone.
   Do not use a kernel extension or a DriverKit physical-device abstraction.
   Keep network/Opus/authentication/default-device policy out of coreaudiod.
   The authenticated session owner feeds an explicit bounded local IPC boundary;
   reject other users/producers, and revoke it before handing over the session.
6. Package/sign the plug-in with the Host and remove it through the normal
   uninstaller. Qualify load/upgrade/removal on macOS27; do not assume a reboot
   or audio-service restart is unnecessary. Never restart audio services on an
   active production machine for this experiment.

## Implementation gates

[x] A: Build a minimal virtual input and non-installing property/IO harness.
       Prove silence, buffer bounds, timing, invalid requests and lifecycle.
       Exercise a synthetic tone on the dedicated development Mac before
       wiring a real Client microphone. No change to working audio output.
[x] B: Add negotiated reverse transport, wire vectors, malformed-peer and
       cancellation/queue bounds tests. Test both directions simultaneously.
[x] C: Add Host injection/selection owner and Client capture/settings/toolbar;
       install/uninstall support and explicit failure reporting.
[x] D: Build matching candidates on approved hosted builders, sign through the
       protected environment, collect checksums and source provenance.
[ ] E: Operator acceptance: Linux/Mac microphones, normal applications, both
       defaults and Manual choices, mute, disconnect/reconnect, logout/login,
       takeover, new-user permissions, microphone unplug/default change,
       crash recovery, packet loss and multi-hour drift/latency. Headphones
       first; speaker echo cancellation is separate qualification, not assumed.

## Current component checkpoint

September 23 integration: schema4 negotiates microphone availability, PLD1
activation/ack controls enforce increasing generations, SDL captures only after
consent plus Host acknowledgement, and Host AudioConverter supplies a bounded
authenticated producer. Both preferences and toolbar mute/state are wired.
Driver signing/receipt ownership/uninstall are implemented. Read the top HANDOFF
section for exact build results and current live fixture state. Post-receive-fix
loss suites pass three consecutive matrices for each sender selection. Gate C
records implementation and component tests, not the application-level input
ownership/lifecycle acceptance required by Gate E.

Gate A: optimized/sanitized portable buffer tests and the native macOS 27 HAL
harness pass. The operator granted normal microphone consent to the distinct
synthetic reader app. Real 48 kHz tone reads pass, including two simultaneous
applications; permission is no longer a blocker. No physical microphone is
recorded by these probes. See HANDOFF for the local IPC and lifecycle results;
this is not end-to-end Client microphone acceptance.

Gate B: optional reverse endpoint ID 1 uses existing KyProto APIs over the same
authenticated connection. Envelope validation, cancellation, bounded queues,
generation changes and encrypted setup/direct round trips pass. Complete builds
exposed an existing receive-drain bug: when config arrived last, completed audio
or video could wait for another packet. Deterministic tests fail before the fix
and pass afterward. Neither FEC policy, sender pacing nor wire format changes.

The non-installing component command on the authorized development Mac is:

```bash
bash scripts/test/build-macos-microphone-probe.sh "$PWD" "$PLANK_MICROPHONE_PROBE_OUTPUT"
```

The output path must be absolute and not exist. This builds tests, an inert
product component, a separate synthetic-tone driver and a reader app, all
ad-hoc signed only for component qualification. It does not install/load either
driver, record a physical microphone, alter defaults/permissions or restart
audio. Live load/unload needs explicit machine authorization and normal OS
microphone consent for the reader app. Do not grant SSH broad microphone access
or confuse the ad-hoc test signature with distribution signing/notarization.

Next: install the signed candidates and perform application-level testing.
Neither a successful build nor a visible device proves applications receive
the intended samples. See HANDOFF for exact current versions and open gates.

## Local IPC design and qualification boundary

macOS 27 hosts the Mach-service-enabled plug-in in Apple's isolated Core Audio
driver helper. The plug-in sandbox cannot inspect arbitrary process identity
with proc_pidinfo, so do not implement writer authorization using a supplied
PID or a permissive custom AudioObject property. Preserve the sandbox.

Use native XPC for authenticated setup/revocation and fixed-size shared memory
for samples. Per-block request/reply IPC was tested and rejected for delivery
gaps. The realtime callback must never wait for XPC, decode Opus, allocate,
access files or acquire a policy lock. A bounded non-realtime control queue
publishes clock state; the producer fills timestamped sample slots. Underflow,
expired producer heartbeat and disconnected control channels supply silence.

Production admission lives in the existing coordinator and authenticated
desktop-worker lifecycle. Only the current signed worker and Apple-signed HAL
helper may join. Each producer lease gets a fresh mapping; activation changes
clear samples without granting old writers access to a new session's mapping.
Root admission expires independently of producer-written data. The driver
copies sanitized bounded blocks into private sample history outside realtime IO.
Host input selection/restoration uses a separate serial queue, so synchronous
HAL calls cannot block coordinator authentication or lease revocation.

Managed synthetic tests pass repeated readers, invalid/duplicate producers,
crash-to-silence and replacement production. Selection restoration, user override,
missing previous input and failure are covered by a fake HAL. Physical input,
real application lifetime and multi-hour drift still require acceptance.
The temporary fixture is removed; no product deployment is implied. Enumeration
briefly stalled after fixture removal/Core Audio restart, then recovered; package
installation/reload behavior remains an explicit gate rather than a claimed pass.

## References

- Apple: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/coreaudio/creating-an-audio-server-driver-plug-in
- Apple: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/audiodriverkit/creating-an-audio-device-driver
- SDL: https://wiki.libsdl.org/SDL3/SDL_OpenAudioDeviceStream

Apple recommends Audio Server plug-ins for software-only virtual devices.
ScreenCaptureKit microphone capture is not a virtual audio-input publisher.
Prefer the established KyProto endpoint APIs; extend no dependency unless its
existing reverse registration genuinely cannot meet the contract.
